  "heading": null,
  "heading_latex": "\\textrm{Euclidean Division}",
  "body_text": null,
  "proof_text": "Let $b > 0$. Consider the set of non-negative integers of the form\n$a - bk$: $$S := \\{a - bk : k \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\land a - bk \\ge 0\\}$$ To\nsee that $S$ is nonempty: if $a \\ge 0$, taking $k = 0$ gives\n$a \\in S$. If $a < 0$, taking $k = a$ gives $a - ba = a(1 - b) \\ge 0$\n(since $b \\ge 1$), so $a(1 - b) \\in S$. Since $S$ is a nonempty subset\nof non-negative integers, by the Well-Ordering Principle it contains a\nleast element $r = a - bq \\ge 0$. We claim $r < b$: if $r \\ge b$, then\n$r - b = a - b(q + 1) \\ge 0$ would belong to $S$, contradicting the\nminimality of $r$. Thus $0 \\le r < b$. To establish uniqueness:\nsuppose $a = bq_1 + r_1 = bq_2 + r_2$ with $0 \\le r_1, r_2 < b$.\nSubtracting yields $b(q_1 - q_2) = r_2 - r_1$, so $b$ divides\n$r_2 - r_1$. Because $-b < r_2 - r_1 < b$, the only multiple of $b$\nstrictly between $-b$ and $b$ is $0$. Therefore $r_2 - r_1 = 0$, so\n$r_1 = r_2$, which in turn forces $q_1 = q_2$.",
  "proof_source": "Proof: \"\"\"Let $b > 0$. Consider the set of non-negative integers of the form\n          $a - bk$: $$S := \\{a - bk : k \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\land a - bk \\ge 0\\}$$ To\n          see that $S$ is nonempty: if $a \\ge 0$, taking $k = 0$ gives\n          $a \\in S$. If $a < 0$, taking $k = a$ gives $a - ba = a(1 - b) \\ge 0$\n          (since $b \\ge 1$), so $a(1 - b) \\in S$. Since $S$ is a nonempty subset\n          of non-negative integers, by the Well-Ordering Principle it contains a\n          least element $r = a - bq \\ge 0$. We claim $r < b$: if $r \\ge b$, then\n          $r - b = a - b(q + 1) \\ge 0$ would belong to $S$, contradicting the\n          minimality of $r$. Thus $0 \\le r < b$. To establish uniqueness:\n          suppose $a = bq_1 + r_1 = bq_2 + r_2$ with $0 \\le r_1, r_2 < b$.\n          Subtracting yields $b(q_1 - q_2) = r_2 - r_1$, so $b$ divides\n          $r_2 - r_1$. Because $-b < r_2 - r_1 < b$, the only multiple of $b$\n          strictly between $-b$ and $b$ is $0$. Therefore $r_2 - r_1 = 0$, so\n          $r_1 = r_2$, which in turn forces $q_1 = q_2$.\"\"\"",
  "page": null,
  "source": "Theorem:\ngiven: a, b is \\integer\nwhere: \\integer.zero \\:integer.less?:/ b\nthen:\n. existsUnique: q is \\integer\n  suchThat:\n  . existsUnique: r is \\integer\n    suchThat:\n    . a = (b \\.integer.*./ q) \\.integer.+./ r\n    . \\integer.zero \\:integer.leq?:/ r\n    . r \\:integer.less?:/ b\nDocumented:\n. called: \"Euclidean Division\"\n. description: \"For $b>0$, there are unique integers $q$ and $r$ such that\n                $a=bq+r$ and $0\\leq r<b$.\"\nProof: \"\"\"Let $b > 0$. Consider the set of non-negative integers of the form\n          $a - bk$: $$S := \\{a - bk : k \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\land a - bk \\ge 0\\}$$ To\n          see that $S$ is nonempty: if $a \\ge 0$, taking $k = 0$ gives\n          $a \\in S$. If $a < 0$, taking $k = a$ gives $a - ba = a(1 - b) \\ge 0$\n          (since $b \\ge 1$), so $a(1 - b) \\in S$. Since $S$ is a nonempty subset\n          of non-negative integers, by the Well-Ordering Principle it contains a\n          least element $r = a - bq \\ge 0$. We claim $r < b$: if $r \\ge b$, then\n          $r - b = a - b(q + 1) \\ge 0$ would belong to $S$, contradicting the\n          minimality of $r$. Thus $0 \\le r < b$. To establish uniqueness:\n          suppose $a = bq_1 + r_1 = bq_2 + r_2$ with $0 \\le r_1, r_2 < b$.\n          Subtracting yields $b(q_1 - q_2) = r_2 - r_1$, so $b$ divides\n          $r_2 - r_1$. Because $-b < r_2 - r_1 < b$, the only multiple of $b$\n          strictly between $-b$ and $b$ is $0$. Therefore $r_2 - r_1 = 0$, so\n          $r_1 = r_2$, which in turn forces $q_1 = q_2$.\"\"\"\nId: \"5dd9f7fc-9815-4816-bc2a-737babe16bf9\"",
